out.push(SAMPLE(['Mrs Khoury said the bureau chief gets the letter, it is two lines, and the chair writes them, because the deadline was the chair\'s, the minute was the chair\'s, and the amendment has to be the chair\'s or it is not an amendment. Mr Shihabi said what must not be said is that he had the minute and did not read it, and that is exactly it and it is true, which is why it is tempting: he had it for twenty-nine minutes and was driving for twenty-nine of them, and a reply that points out that he had it has told a man that a deadline he could not meet was his own fault. Mrs Oueidat said this is the first time in the book that the chair is at fault, and Mr Zahreddine said the fault is small, mechanical and entirely his, because he set a deadline of eleven and he knew where the man was, and a route that one of six people cannot use is not a route, it is a route for five. Dr Qabbani asked whether the decision stands, and Mrs Khoury said the decision stands and the minute is amended, and those are two answers to two questions: the figure is published with its denominator from 1 March, which nobody has argued against, and what is amended is the record of agreement. Mr Attar said and the reason is a position, and Mrs Oueidat said that is the finding of the cycle and nobody has noticed it for a fortnight, because publication depends on the count being settled is an argument about item 1 and it has never been put to anybody. Mrs Rizk said so it goes on the next agenda, and first item, by name. Mr Tayara asked what happens to the deadline, and it becomes noon or the start of the next call, whichever is later, because an amendment that fixes one minute and leaves the mechanism alone is an apology with a date on it. Mrs Khoury said the change costs the chair the thing he most values, which is the hour. Mrs Tarabishi asked whether the regional editor is involved, and he is copied and not addressed. Mrs Ajouz asked whether the sending log should go in, and Mr Zahreddine said the log goes in, and this is the one place where a fact about somebody else\'s conduct helps them. And Mr Shihabi said the thing he will take away is the fortnight: for fourteen days four people discussed whether a man had agreed to something, and nobody looked at a log that answered it in nine words or asked what his reason was.']));
out.push(A('⚠ **Three lines to carry out of the unit, in this order.** **First: the fault is the chair\'s, and it is a deadline, not a judgement.** **Second: the log goes in, produced by the chair, about the man complaining — which is the whole of the good faith in the document and costs nine words.** **Third, and last: the fortnight.** **Four people spent fourteen days on whether a man had agreed, and nobody asked what his reason was** — **and his reason turned out to be the only position anybody held about item 1.**'));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('**1** ⚠ _You had the minute_ → **true, and it makes an impossible deadline his fault.**   **2** **One answer for two questions** → **the half you did not address comes back.**   **3** ⚠ **The minute amended and the deadline left alone** → **the same failure in March.**   **4** **A letter to the senior editor** → **a two-line correction becomes a question about three people\'s judgement.**   **5** ⚠ **The log left out** → **the one fact that helps him, withheld by the only person who had it.**   **6** **The reason treated as an excuse** → **it is a position, and it goes first on the next agenda.**'));
